"""Extra state-specific section for state pages that score lowest on the location uniqueness test.
Each entry: (h2, [paragraphs]). Added in Build 16."""

STATE_EXTRA = {
"SD": ("East River farms, West River hills: two pest seasons", [
 "South Dakota's pest calendar splits at the Missouri River. East River, around Sioux Falls, Brookings, Watertown and Aberdeen, harvest season in the corn and soybean fields drives field mice and deer mice into houses, garages and grain bins, and shelterbelts around farmsteads shelter boxelder bugs that move onto siding each fall. American dog ticks are the most common tick in the grasslands.",
 "West River, the Black Hills went through a long mountain pine beetle outbreak from the late 1990s into the 2010s, and the forest edges around Rapid City, Spearfish and Custer still bring deer mice, wasps and wildlife close to cabins. The Sturgis Motorcycle Rally fills campgrounds, motels and short-term rentals every August, so lodging owners should inspect for bed bugs after the rush. Hantavirus cases, linked to deer mice, have been reported in the state.",
 "The big Missouri River reservoirs, from Lake Oahe near Pierre to Lewis and Clark Lake near Yankton, draw anglers, campers and cabin owners, and their shorelines and backwaters produce heavy mosquito and midge hatches in summer. South Dakota often reports some of the highest West Nile virus rates per person in the country."]),
"KS": ("Recluses, termites and the Flint Hills", [
 "Kansas sits in the heart of brown recluse country, and the spiders are common in houses, garages and storage across the eastern two-thirds of the state, from Kansas City and Lawrence to Topeka, Manhattan and Wichita. Eastern subterranean termites are found statewide, with the heaviest pressure in the wetter east, and many Kansas home sales include a termite inspection.",
 "In the tallgrass prairie of the Flint Hills, ranchers burn pastures each spring, and ticks and chiggers are heavy in the regrowth through early summer. Wheat and grain storage across western Kansas means mice and rats near elevators, bins and farm shops. Severe spring storms and hail can open roofs and siding, giving mice, squirrels and wasps new ways in.",
 "Kansas sits in Tornado Alley, and spring storms and hail damage roofs, siding and soffits across Wichita, Topeka and the suburbs of Kansas City. Repaired roofs can leave small gaps that squirrels, raccoons, bats and wasps use, so a roofline check after storm repairs is worth doing."]),
"NH": ("Lakes Region camps, the Seacoast and the White Mountains", [
 "Many New Hampshire homes sit empty for part of the year, from summer camps on Lake Winnipesaukee and Squam Lake to ski houses near North Conway and Waterville Valley. Closed-up camps are a favorite winter home for deer mice and red squirrels, and carpenter ants nest in the damp sills, decks and log walls of lakeside cabins.",
 "New Hampshire has one of the highest Lyme disease rates in the country, and blacklegged ticks are common in the southern and Seacoast counties. Eastern equine encephalitis has caused human cases in the state in some years. Along the Seacoast near Portsmouth, browntail moth caterpillars have spread in recent years; their tiny hairs cause itchy rashes, so nests in oaks and fruit trees should be removed by professionals.",
 "New Hampshire's ski lodges, inns and short-term rentals in the White Mountains and the Lakes Region host a steady flow of visitors, and bed bugs can arrive with luggage. Lodging owners do best with routine inspections between busy weekends and mattress encasements in guest rooms."]),
"WY": ("Ranches, mountain towns and the long Wyoming winter", [
 "Wyoming's cold, windy winters push deer mice and house mice into homes, ranch buildings, cabins and campers every fall, and deer mice can carry hantavirus, so droppings should be wetted with disinfectant before cleanup. Cluster flies and boxelder bugs gather in attics and walls of homes in Casper, Cheyenne, Laramie and Sheridan.",
 "In the mountains around Jackson, Cody and the gateway towns to Yellowstone and Grand Teton, Rocky Mountain wood ticks are active in spring, and the hotels, lodges and short-term rentals that fill each summer need regular bed bug checks. Bark beetle outbreaks killed large areas of pine forest in southern Wyoming, and yellowjackets build in decks and log homes during the short summer.",
 "Ranch country brings its own pests: stable flies, house flies and horse flies breed in manure and wet hay around barns and corrals, and pack rats nest in sheds, haystacks and parked equipment. Grain and feed storage on ranches attracts mice year-round."]),
"WV": ("Hollows, rivers and the Eastern Panhandle", [
 "West Virginia's steep hollows and river valleys keep homes damp, which suits subterranean termites, carpenter ants and moisture pests like millipedes and camel crickets in basements. Major floods, including the June 2016 flooding in Greenbrier, Kanawha and nearby counties, left many homes with water-damaged framing that termites later found.",
 "The Eastern Panhandle around Martinsburg and Charles Town was hit hard by brown marmorated stink bugs, which swarm homes and orchards each fall. West Virginia also reports some of the most La Crosse encephalitis cases in the country, a mosquito-borne illness spread mainly by tree hole mosquitoes that breed in tree cavities, tires and containers, so emptying standing water around wooded homes matters.",
 "West Virginia's hunting camps, cabins and vacation rentals in the mountains, from the New River Gorge to Snowshoe and Canaan Valley, sit empty for long stretches, and deer mice, flying squirrels and carpenter bees move in. Deer mice in closed cabins should be cleaned up with wet methods and gloves."]),
"ND": ("Red River floods, prairie winters and the Bakken", [
 "North Dakota's prairie winters are among the coldest in the lower 48, and mice and boxelder bugs press into homes and farm buildings every fall. Along the Red River in Fargo and Grand Forks, repeated floods, including the 1997 flood that swamped Grand Forks, have left basements that need careful moisture and pest control. Cass County runs a vector control program for the Fargo area.",
 "Summer mosquitoes can be intense across the state's wetlands and prairie potholes, and North Dakota often has one of the highest West Nile virus case rates per person in the country. In the western oil country around Williston, Watford City and Dickinson, the Bakken boom brought crew camps, apartments and fast-built housing, where bed bugs and mice spread with frequent turnover."]),
"NV": ("Two Nevadas: the Las Vegas Valley and the Great Basin", [
 "Pest control in southern Nevada and northern Nevada can feel like two different states. In the Las Vegas Valley, extreme heat brings bark scorpions, American cockroaches and roof rats, and the Southern Nevada Health District has found invasive Aedes aegypti mosquitoes in recent years. Hotels and casinos with constant guest turnover need ongoing bed bug programs.",
 "In Reno, Sparks, Carson City and Elko, the high desert brings cold winters, yellowjackets, voles and deer mice instead. Mormon crickets, a flightless katydid, have had large outbreaks in rural northern Nevada, swarming roads and ranch yards. Hantavirus is a concern around cabins and outbuildings in the north. Pest control companies statewide are licensed by the Nevada Department of Agriculture."]),
"OK": ("Tornado Alley, red dirt and recluses", [
 "Oklahoma combines brown recluse spiders, which are common in homes statewide, with some of the heaviest subterranean termite pressure in the central U.S. Termite inspections are a routine part of home sales from Oklahoma City and Edmond to Tulsa, Norman and Lawton. Red imported fire ants have spread into parts of southern Oklahoma.",
 "Ticks are a serious concern: Oklahoma has high rates of tick-borne illnesses like ehrlichiosis, spread mostly by lone star ticks in grassy and wooded areas. Severe storms and tornadoes damage roofs and siding every spring, opening paths for mice, squirrels, wasps and moisture pests. Lakes like Grand, Eufaula and Texoma bring mosquitoes to lake homes."]),
"KY": ("Bluegrass, caves and the coalfields", [
 "Kentucky's limestone karst, the same geology behind Mammoth Cave, keeps soils damp across much of the state, and eastern subterranean termites are active statewide. Brown recluse spiders are common in homes, and carpenter bees drill into porches and barns each spring. The University of Kentucky's entomology program is a respected source of homeowner guidance on bed bugs and other pests.",
 "In the eastern coalfields, the July 2022 floods damaged thousands of homes in Breathitt, Knott, Perry and nearby counties, and flood-soaked framing is prone to termites, mold and moisture pests. Across Louisville, Lexington and Northern Kentucky, stink bugs and lady beetles gather on homes each fall, and mice move into older brick homes."]),
"NE": ("Cropland, river towns and the Sandhills", [
 "Most Nebraskans live in the east, from Omaha and Lincoln to Grand Island and Kearney, where eastern subterranean termites and brown recluse spiders are common. Grain bins and farm buildings across the state's corn and soybean country attract mice and rats after harvest, and boxelder bugs swarm homes in fall.",
 "The Platte and Missouri rivers breed mosquitoes, and Nebraska had one of the country's largest West Nile virus outbreaks in 2003. The March 2019 floods damaged homes and farms along the Missouri, Platte and Elkhorn rivers. In the Sandhills and western Nebraska, ticks are active in grassland, and ranch homes see deer mice and wasps."]),
"RI": ("Coastal cottages, mill villages and triple-deckers", [
 "Rhode Island packs a lot into a small state: triple-deckers in Providence, Pawtucket and Central Falls, mill villages along the Blackstone and Pawtuxet rivers, and summer cottages from Narragansett to Westerly and Block Island. Older wood homes see carpenter ants and subterranean termites, while seasonal cottages fill with mice over the winter.",
 "Blacklegged ticks are common, and Rhode Island has one of the higher Lyme disease rates in the country. Eastern equine encephalitis caused human cases in Rhode Island during the 2019 outbreak, and the state tracks mosquitoes each summer. Winter moth caterpillars have defoliated trees in parts of the state, and rats are a steady complaint in dense Providence neighborhoods."]),
"MT": ("Big Sky cabins, mountain towns and wildlife", [
 "Montana's cabins, ranches and mountain homes around Missoula, Bozeman, Whitefish and Helena sit close to forest and open land. Deer mice move into cabins and outbuildings each fall, and hantavirus cases have been reported in the state, so wet cleaning of droppings is important. Cluster flies and boxelder bugs pack into attics and walls before winter.",
 "Rocky Mountain wood ticks are active in spring, and Colorado tick fever and other tick-borne illnesses occur in the state. Bark beetle outbreaks have killed large stands of pine. Yellowjackets build big nests by late summer, and bears, raccoons and skunks are drawn to trash and bird feeders in towns near the mountains. Billings and Great Falls see bed bugs in apartments and lodging."]),
"NM": ("High desert, Chihuahuan desert and mountain villages", [
 "New Mexico has some of the most distinctive pest risks in the country. The state reports more human plague cases than any other, usually linked to fleas on rock squirrels and prairie dogs, and it also records many hantavirus cases tied to deer mice. Both make careful rodent control and cleanup important around homes, sheds and cabins from Albuquerque and Santa Fe to Gallup and Taos.",
 "In the south, around Las Cruces and the Mesilla Valley, the Chihuahuan Desert brings scorpions, kissing bugs and heat-driven cockroaches, and pecan orchards attract roof rats. Subterranean termites are active statewide, attacking framing and trim even in adobe-style homes. Mountain communities in Ruidoso and the Sangre de Cristos see wasps, deer mice and wildlife."]),
"SC": ("Lowcountry, Midlands and Upstate: three pest climates", [
 "South Carolina's pests change from the coast to the mountains. In the Lowcountry, from Charleston and Mount Pleasant to Beaufort and Hilton Head, the humid salt air supports Formosan subterranean termites alongside native termites, and salt marshes breed mosquitoes and no-see-ums. Many coastal homes sit on raised foundations over damp crawlspaces, and hurricanes from Hugo in 1989 to more recent storms have left flood-soaked framing behind.",
 "The Midlands around Columbia are hot and sandy, with fire ants in lawns and pastures and big outdoor cockroaches. The Upstate around Greenville and Spartanburg sits in the Blue Ridge foothills, where kudzu bugs and brown marmorated stink bugs swarm homes in spring and fall. Pest control businesses in South Carolina are licensed through Clemson University's Department of Pesticide Regulation, an unusual arrangement where the state's land-grant university is the regulator."]),
"PA": ("Lanternflies, stink bugs and Lyme in Pennsylvania", [
 "Pennsylvania has been at the front line of two invasive insects. The brown marmorated stink bug was first collected in the U.S. near Allentown in the late 1990s and now gathers on homes statewide every fall. The spotted lanternfly was found in Berks County in 2014, and the Pennsylvania Department of Agriculture now runs a quarantine across many counties, asking residents and businesses to check vehicles and goods before moving them out of infested areas.",
 "Pennsylvania also reports some of the highest numbers of Lyme disease cases in the country, with blacklegged ticks common from the Poconos to the suburbs of Philadelphia and Pittsburgh. Its older housing stock, from Philadelphia rowhomes to Pittsburgh hillside homes and small-town brick houses, gives mice, carpenter ants and subterranean termites plenty to work with."]),
"MO": ("Recluses, ticks and two big rivers in Missouri", [
 "Missouri sits in the heart of brown recluse country, and the spiders are common in homes from St. Louis and Kansas City to Springfield, Columbia and the Ozarks. Missouri also has some of the highest rates of tick-borne illness in the nation, including ehrlichiosis and Rocky Mountain spotted fever, spread mostly by lone star ticks in the Ozark woods and grasslands.",
 "The Missouri and Mississippi rivers meet near St. Louis, and floods like the Great Flood of 1993 and the 2019 floods soaked basements and riverfront towns, leaving moisture pests and termite-prone wood behind. Eastern subterranean termites are found statewide, and older brick homes in St. Louis and Kansas City see mice and rats each fall."]),
"IA": ("Floods, farm fields and fall invaders in Iowa", [
 "Iowa's river cities have a long flood history, from the 1993 floods that knocked out Des Moines' water supply to the 2008 floods in Cedar Rapids and Iowa City and the 2019 Mississippi River flood in Davenport. Each flood leaves wet basements that draw millipedes, centipedes, springtails and mice, and damp framing that subterranean termites can later find.",
 "Across the state's corn and soybean country, harvest season pushes field mice into homes, garages and grain bins, and boxelder bugs and Asian lady beetles swarm sunny walls each fall. The August 2020 derecho toppled trees and damaged roofs across central and eastern Iowa, opening attics to squirrels, raccoons and bats. Iowa State University's extension service is a trusted source of homeowner pest guidance."]),
"ME": ("Browntail moths, ticks and camps in Maine", [
 "Maine faces a pest few other states do: the browntail moth, whose caterpillars shed toxic hairs that cause rashes and breathing irritation. Outbreaks have been heaviest in southern and midcoast Maine, from Portland and Brunswick to Augusta and Waterville, and the state tracks their spread each year. Maine also has one of the highest Lyme disease rates in the country, with blacklegged ticks spreading north and inland.",
 "Many Maine homes and camps on lakes and the coast sit empty for part of the year, and deer mice, red squirrels and carpenter ants move in when they're closed. Pest control applicators in Maine are licensed by the Maine Board of Pesticides Control, which also publishes guidance on browntail moth treatments near water."]),
"VT": ("Cluster flies, carpenter ants and Vermont winters", [
 "Vermont's old farmhouses, village homes and ski-country condos face long, snowy winters, and cluster flies are one of the most common complaints. They develop in earthworms in pastures and lawns, then gather by the hundreds in attics and wall voids each fall, crawling out on sunny winter days. Carpenter ants nest in damp sills, porches and log homes, and deer mice move into camps and sugarhouses.",
 "Blacklegged ticks have spread rapidly in Vermont over the past two decades, and Lyme disease and anaplasmosis cases have risen, especially in the southern and western counties. Pest control applicators are certified by the Vermont Agency of Agriculture, Food and Markets."]),
"IN": ("Termites, recluses and the Ohio River in Indiana", [
 "Eastern subterranean termites are active across Indiana, from Fort Wayne and South Bend in the north to Indianapolis and Evansville in the south, and termite inspections are a routine part of home sales. Southern Indiana, along the Ohio River from Evansville to New Albany and Jeffersonville, is warmer and more humid, with brown recluse spiders, heavier mosquito seasons and river floods that soak basements.",
 "The emerald ash borer killed millions of ash trees across Indiana after it arrived in the early 2000s, and dead trees and stumps became homes for carpenter ants and wildlife. Northern Indiana gets lake-effect snow off Lake Michigan, and mice, cluster flies and lady beetles move indoors each fall. Purdue University's entomology extension is a widely used resource for homeowners."]),
}
